imud-utils (1.10.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * imud-imutest runs the IMU's built-in self-test and grades the per-axis
    response against the datasheet window. It needs no motion and no
    operator, so it runs under --passive.  This is the only passive check
    that distinguishes a live sensing element from a dead one: at rest
    both read about zero and pass every other check. Implemented for
    ism330dhcx, and for mpu9250/mpu9255/mpu6500 where the response is
    recorded rather than graded because TDK publishes no window for it.
    Other drivers structurally skip it and stay clearable.
  * imud builds and runs on big-endian hosts. The binary packet and the
    .imucap file are converted field by field instead of memcpy'd in host
    order, so they stay little-endian whatever the machine, and the Debian
    packages are now Architecture: linux-any.  No wire-format change of
    its own: the bytes a little-endian host emits are identical to before.
    The packet does move to v18 in this release, for a separate change.
  * Wire format v18: the packet grows 276 to 288 bytes with flags_ext, a
    second 32-bit flag word, and eight reserved bytes. Every existing
    field keeps its offset. Its first bit says no magnetometer is
    configured.  Breaking: consumers reject a wire version they do not
    know, so deploy the daemon and its consumers together. flags had
    assigned all sixteen bits, which is what forced the new word — a
    consumer must ignore bits it does not recognise, and a later flag then
    needs no further bump. The imud-arduino client needs its own update.
  * NMEA withholds $HCHDM, $HCHDG and $HCHDT, and leaves $PASHR's heading
    field null, while no magnetometer is being fused. An uncalibrated
    magnetometer still publishes all of them.  A rig with no magnetometer,
    or one that has stopped delivering, used to publish a dead-reckoned
    heading as magnetic heading; no heading sentence has a field that
    could qualify it. Consumers now get no heading rather than a wrong
    one. $TIROT and $IIXDR are unaffected, and imud-mon shows heading as
    --.
  * imud-imutest's guided gyro and spin phases integrated every angle
    1000x low. A commanded 90 degree turn reported 0.1 degrees, and the
    tool blamed the driver's FIFO for it.  Affects 1.9.0 and 1.9.1.
    Reports from those versions show gyro.*.sign, gyro.*.scale,
    imu.direct.gyro and spin.frame_agreement failing or skipping on
    hardware that was working correctly; re-run to grade the part.
  * imud-imutest now shows 'Enter when done' while a guided turn is
    collecting. The hint only appeared when there was nothing else on the
    line, so operators let every turn run to its timeout.
  * imud-imutest reported the mpu9250/mpu9255 pair as not holding its
    configuration through a run. A driver's probe() now leaves an already-
    configured part alone, which is what the tool's repeated probes had
    been disturbing.  The finding was the tool's, not the driver's:
    reports show a FAIL on imu.regs.after_run and a WARN on
    imu.init.idempotent, both naming USER_CTRL. The daemon probes once,
    before it configures anything, and was never affected. rm3100's probe
    had the same fault and is fixed with it.
  * The armhf packages are now built on Raspbian at the ARMv6 baseline, so
    they run on every Raspberry Pi that boots a 32-bit userland.
    Previously they were built to Debian's ARMv7 baseline.  On a Pi 1 or
    Pi Zero/Zero W the previous packages installed without complaint and
    then died on an illegal instruction, because both ports report the
    same "armhf" architecture. Those boards can now install from the apt
    repository. The arm64 packages are unchanged.

 -- Richard Simpson <richcreations@gmail.com>  Sat, 05 Sep 2026 09:31:46 -0700

imud-utils (1.9.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * imud-imutest no longer recommends clearing experimental on a gyro that
    produced nothing: the per-axis sign check now requires a quarter of
    the commanded turn, so a dead data path fails instead of passing on
    noise.  A report generated before this could pass all three gyro sign
    checks on integrated noise while the scale checks failed against the
    same value. Re-run imud-imutest for any driver cleared on such a
    report.

 -- Richard Simpson <richcreations@gmail.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 16:54:04 -0700

imud-utils (1.9.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Sample rates are carried in milli-Hz throughout, so the advertised
    rates are now the silicon's own. The ISM330DHCX ladder is 6664/2^n,
    making its bottom rung 13.016 Hz where the datasheet prints 12.5.
    odr_hz accepts fractional rates. A rate that rounds to zero is
    refused.
  * imud-imutest measures the magnetometer the way the daemon reads it,
    and counts data-ready edges against the drain rather than a timer of
    its own.

 -- Richard Simpson <richcreations@gmail.com>  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 04:48:20 -0700

imud-utils (1.8.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: imud-cal could write, and would load, a calibration that made
    the filter useless. strtof converts "nan", "inf" and "1e999" without
    complaint and the loader looked no further than "did any digits
    convert", so a gyro bias of nan loaded clean and left the daemon
    publishing NaN attitude forever. The loop closed entirely inside
    imud's own tooling: cal_write formats with %.8f, which round-trips
    a NaN straight back out, so a degenerate fit wrote a file the daemon
    then reloaded. Loading now names the offending field, and writing
    refuses -- validating BEFORE the file is opened for truncation, so a
    rejected write leaves the previous calibration intact on disk.
  * Fix: a calibration that is finite but enormous is refused too. A
    soft_iron element of 3.33e37 is finite, so the check above accepted
    it, and soft_iron x (sample - hard_iron) for a sample of ZERO is
    -3.8e38, which overflows a float. imud-cal now asks only whether the
    calibration arithmetic survives an ordinary sample, never whether a
    value is physically sensible: the probes are 0 and +/-1000, above
    the full scale of every sensor in the tree, so a calibration has to
    be some thirty-five orders of magnitude out before it trips and a
    legitimate fit cannot be rejected.
  * Fix: imud-imutest accepted garbage in its numeric options. --odr,
    --imu-addr, --int-gpio and the rest used atoi/atof, which report no
    error, and they failed three different ways: a bad --odr was
    silently discarded, a bad --imu-addr became 0 and TOOK, probing I2C
    address 0, and a bad --int-gpio became 0 -- which the man page
    documents as "skips the interrupt check", so the check the operator
    asked for was skipped while the report still said PASS. All twelve
    conversions now reject a bad value as a usage error.
  * imud-mon shows the new FLAG_STATE_RESET (bit 14) as R: the daemon's
    filter found a non-finite value in its own state and reset itself.
    It stays set until the filter re-converges, so it is visible at
    imud-mon's refresh rate rather than for a single packet.
  * imud-mon and imud-imutest inherit the config parser's new strictness
    (see the imud changelog): an over-long, out-of-range or non-finite
    value is refused rather than truncated or wrapped. imud-mon still
    runs on defaults when its config cannot be parsed, and the rejected
    key now genuinely keeps its default rather than a truncated value.
  * No wire-format change: the packet stays v17 / 276 bytes.

 -- Richard Simpson <richcreations@gmail.com>  Wed, 05 Aug 2026 12:00:00 -0700

imud-utils (1.8.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: imud-imutest wrote its report to a file literally named
    "imud-im", and reported that name, whenever --report was not given.
    The auto-generated default path was built against the size of a
    pointer rather than the buffer, truncating it to seven characters.
    An explicit --report <path> was never affected.
  * imud-imutest now resolves the sample rate the same way the daemon
    does, so it measures the part against the rate the daemon would
    actually program rather than one the daemon never selects. See the
    imud changelog for the ODR fix this belongs to.
  * BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: imud-mon and imud-status reach the daemon through
    the imud group, not gpio. On Raspberry Pi OS the default user is in
    gpio and will now find the daemon unreachable until
    `sudo adduser "$USER" imud`.
  * Harden: every fd both tools open is close-on-exec, including
    accepted sockets, which POSIX does not carry FD_CLOEXEC across.
  * No wire-format change: the packet stays v17 / 276 bytes.

 -- Richard Simpson <richcreations@gmail.com>  Mon, 03 Aug 2026 12:00:00 -0700

imud-utils (1.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New tool imud-imutest: validates a sensor driver against real hardware
    and writes a Markdown report suitable for attaching to a bug report.
    Unlike imud-mon it talks to the sensor over I2C, so it must run on the
    machine the sensor is attached to, with the daemon stopped. This adds a
    libgpiod dependency to the package.
  * Reports are written 0644 rather than 0666 narrowed by the umask.

 -- Richard Simpson <richcreations@gmail.com>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 22:00:00 -0700

